with the demo. First of all, we'll use Burp Scanner in Burp Suite Professional to test for directory

11
00:01:05,800 --> 00:01:13,580
traversal vulnerabilities. Once the lab has launched, identify a request you want to investigate.

12
00:01:13,580 --> 00:01:19,320
For this demo, we're going to use a request for one of the product images, which I can find

13
00:01:19,320 --> 00:01:33,700
here in my proxy HTTP history tab. Right-click the request and select Do Active Scan and Burp Scanner

14
00:01:33,700 --> 00:01:48,000
will begin to audit the request. You can review the Issue Activity panel on Burp's dashboard or in the details for the scan task to identify any directory

15
00:01:48,000 --> 00:02:00,680
traversal issues that Burp Scanner flags. Alternatively, we can use Burp Intruder to test for directory traversal vulnerabilities.

16
00:02:00,680 --> 00:02:07,680
This process also allows you to closely investigate any issues Burp Scanner has identified.

17
00:02:07,680 --> 00:02:16,680
From the proxy HTTP history tab, identify the request you want to investigate. We'll use the same request as we did for the first example.

18
00:02:16,680 --> 00:02:28,680
Right-click the request and choose Send to Intruder. Switch to the Intruder tab and highlight the parameter you want to test.

19
00:02:28,680 --> 00:02:41,680
Click on Add to mark it as a payload position. On the Payload tab, if you're using Bepp Suite Professional, you can select the built-in Fuzzing Path Traversal

20
00:02:41,680 --> 00:02:49,680
Word List or create your own. If you're using Community, you'll need to manually add a list. Click Start Attack.

21
00:02:49,680 --> 00:03:02,680
Intruder will send a request for each Fuz string on the list. When the attack has finished, check the results for any interesting behaviour, such as responses with a longer length.

22
00:03:02,680 --> 00:03:06,680
These may contain data that has been returned from the requested file.

